#|
What this config does:

Remaps:

- caps      -> Left Ctrl (plain remap, no tap/hold behavior)
- grv       -> tap: backtick/grave; hold: activate the `arrows` layer
- hjkl      -> left/down/up/right, but only while the `arrows` layer is active

Home row mods:

- asdf      -> tap: themselves; hold: option/control/cmd/shift
- jkl;      -> tap: themselves; hold: shift/cmd/control/option (mirrors asdf)

  Tap then re-press within 200ms to hold the letter and auto-repeat instead.
  Same-hand pairs stack: hold j+k past 200ms for cmd+shift.

Chords, each pressed together within 50ms:

- q + w     -> esc
- x + c     -> cmd+c
- c + v     -> cmd+v
- h + j     -> +          (inactive on the arrows layer)
- , + .     -> backtick
- tab + q   -> shift+tab
|#

;; Text after double-semicolons are single-line comments.

#|
One defcfg entry may be added, which is used for configuration key-pairs. These
configurations change kanata's behaviour at a more global level than the other
configuration entries.
|#

(defcfg
  #|
  This configuration will process all keys pressed inside of kanata, even if
  they are not mapped in defsrc. This is so that certain actions can activate
  at the right time for certain input sequences. By default, unmapped keys are
  not processed through kanata due to a Windows issue related to AltGr. If you
  use AltGr in your keyboard, you will likely want to follow the simple.kbd
  file while unmapping lctl and ralt from defsrc.
  |#
  process-unmapped-keys yes

  ;; Kanata refuses to start with defchordsv2 unless this is enabled.
  concurrent-tap-hold yes
)

(defsrc
  caps   grv
  a      s      d      f      h    j      k      l      ;
  lsft   rsft
)

(deflayer default
  lctl   @grv
  @a-alt @s-ctl @d-cmd @f-sft _    @j-sft @k-cmd @l-ctl @scln-alt
  _      _
)

(deflayer arrows
  _      _
  _      _      _      _      left down   up     rght   _
  _      _
)

#|
Which hand each key belongs to, so the home row mods below can tell an
intentional cross-hand chord apart from a same-hand typing roll.
|#
(defhands
  (left  q w e r t a s d f g z x c v b)
  (right y u i o p h j k l ; n m , . /)
)

#|
A hold commits to its modifier once an opposite-hand key is pressed *and
released* while it is still down. Waiting for that release is what keeps typing
safe: rolling "aj" releases the a before the j, which resolves as a plain tap,
while deliberately holding a and tapping j gives option+j.

(same-hand ignore) is what lets same-hand pairs like j+k stack into cmd+shift —
a same-hand press neither commits nor cancels the decision, it just keeps
waiting, so both keys reach their timeout and (timeout hold) turns both into
modifiers. A quick same-hand roll still types letters because both keys release
well before 200ms elapses.

tap-repress-timeout skips the hold decision on a quick re-press, so tap-then-hold
auto-repeats the letter.
|#
(deftemplate hrm (key mod)
  (tap-hold-opposite-hand-release 200 $key $mod
    (timeout hold)
    (same-hand ignore)
    (tap-repress-timeout 200))
)

(defalias
  grv (tap-hold-press 200 200 grv (layer-toggle arrows))

  a-alt    (t! hrm a lalt)
  s-ctl    (t! hrm s lctl)
  d-cmd    (t! hrm d lmet)
  f-sft    (t! hrm f lsft)

  j-sft    (t! hrm j rsft)
  k-cmd    (t! hrm k rmet)
  l-ctl    (t! hrm l rctl)
  scln-alt (t! hrm ; ralt)
)

#|
Chords fire before layer handling, so these keys don't need to be in defsrc —
process-unmapped-keys already routes them through kanata. That ordering is also
why h+j beats the home row mod on j. The 50ms windows are tight on purpose: they
keep ordinary rolls (a fast "qwerty", or "cv" in "cvs") from firing a chord.

h+j is disabled on the arrows layer, where those two keys are left and down.
|#
(defchordsv2
  (q w)   esc   50 first-release ()
  (x c)   M-c   50 first-release ()
  (c v)   M-v   50 first-release ()
  (h j)   +     50 first-release (arrows)
  (, .)   grv   50 first-release ()
  (tab q) S-tab 50 first-release ()
)
